WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.360
Every protein is built from about 20
small molecules: the amino acids.

2
00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:07.520
The simplest is glycine.

3
00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:10.460
Add glycine to an acid, and it reacts.

4
00:00:10.460 --> 00:00:13.920
Add it to a strong base instead,
and it reacts again.

5
00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:16.980
One molecule, two opposite partners.

6
00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:21.760
So how can one small molecule
react with both acids and bases?

7
00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:24.300
In this mission, you'll
meet the amino acids,

8
00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:27.500
built around one carbon
atom: the alpha carbon.

9
00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:32.360
You'll see that glycine and alanine
differ only in their side chain.

10
00:00:32.360 --> 00:00:36.940
Then you'll predict exactly which
form of glycine is present in acid.

11
00:00:36.940 --> 00:00:42.920
Your mission: explain how one small molecule
can react with both acids and bases.